    "description": "Iran’s economic history connects agricultural production, urban crafts, commercial networks, monetary institutions, and the development of petroleum. Its central questions concern how resources were produced, exchanged, taxed, and distributed across changing political systems.",
    "tags": ["Economic history", "Trade", "Agriculture", "Industrialization", "Banking", "Petroleum"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-26T14:41:53Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-26T14:41:53Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Iran’s Economic History: Trade, Industry, Banking and Oil | Iran, Unfolded",
      "description": "Explore the changing relationships between farming, markets, money, industry, oil, and state power in Iran’s economic history."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "production-and-power",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Production, exchange, and power",
        "content": "Iran’s economic history is best approached through the connections between households, markets, and political authority. Under the Safavids, the dynasty that established its rule in the early sixteenth century, villages remained fundamental units of agricultural production and taxation. Urban workshops supplied everyday needs as well as the court’s demand for textiles, manuscripts, architecture, and other luxury goods. Economic change therefore involved both production and the institutions that claimed its proceeds. [s1]\n\nThis perspective also makes regional differences visible. A court-centered city, an agricultural village, and a commercial port participated in different markets and faced different demands. The growth of court consumption in Isfahan, the Safavid capital, benefited selected artisans and merchants while drawing resources toward royal establishments. Commercial opportunity and political privilege could reinforce each other. They should not be treated as interchangeable measures of prosperity for the population as a whole. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "markets-and-money",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Markets, trade, and monetary evidence",
        "content": "International exchange attracts attention because it left accounts of silk, merchants, and distant destinations. Yet Willem Floor’s study of Safavid commerce emphasizes the greater importance of local trade in necessities. Food, clothing, utensils, and animals moved between towns and their surrounding districts. Gulf shipping expanded without simply extinguishing overland exchange with India. The relationship between maritime and inland commerce changed over time rather than following a single national trajectory. [s2]\n\nMoney provides another route into this history. Sasanian silver drachms—coins issued by the dynasty that preceded the Islamic conquest—served taxation and state expenditure, including military payments. Copper coins also supported everyday exchange. Nikolaus Schindel warns that collections dominated by silver can give a misleading picture: excavations reveal a more substantial role for copper. Surviving prestigious objects are therefore an imperfect guide to ordinary economic life. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "banks-and-factories",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Banks, factories, and a stronger central state",
        "content": "Modern banking changed the means through which savings, payments, and government borrowing were organized. Bank Melli, Iran’s national bank, developed a branch network and a national banknote system that helped reduce barriers to transfers between regions. Its importance extended beyond accepting deposits: it became an instrument of public finance and institutional integration. This history also involved efforts to reduce dependence on foreign financial institutions. [s4]\n\nIndustrialization introduced machinery and larger workplaces into an economy where crafts had previously predominated. Hassan Hakimian’s account of the period from 1925 to 1953 identifies textiles as a particularly important area of industrial employment growth. Government assistance, purchasing policies, and financial support helped sustain factories. A factory’s establishment consequently does not by itself demonstrate commercial independence or durable competitiveness. [s5]\n\nPetroleum developed alongside these industries but initially had limited connections with domestic manufacturing. Its geographical and ownership concentration made it an industrial enclave: rising extraction and employment did not automatically generate equally strong demand for locally manufactured equipment and supplies. [s5]",
        "source_ids": ["s4", "s5"]
      },
      {
        "id": "development-and-distribution",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Development and its distribution",
        "content": "During the later Pahlavi period, development policy combined infrastructure, industrial protection, bank credit, and agricultural reform. The land reform initiated in 1962 redistributed qualifying holdings to sharecroppers, but its exemptions and subsequent phases complicate any simple account of a uniform rural transformation. In economist M. Hashem Pesaran’s interpretation, industrial expansion and improving living standards coexisted with persistent inequalities between sectors and regions. [s6]\n\nThe revolution of 1979 changed economic institutions and ownership, while the war with Iraq, sanctions, and oil-market fluctuations imposed additional pressures. Vahid Nowshirvani’s historical study stresses both disruption and continuity, especially the continuing importance of oil exports. Its account describes the early Islamic Republic and should not be read as a description of present-day regulation. [s7]\n\nThe branches below examine these subjects at different scales: routes and merchants; extraction and revenue; industrial workplaces; banks; coins and currency; and foreign trade. Together they encourage a distinction between increased output, stronger state finances, and improvements in everyday livelihoods. The historical studies cited here show why those outcomes require separate evidence. [s2][s5][s6][s7]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s5", "s6", "s7"]
      }
    ],
